// Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus), 2026-10-27, Daniel Fontaine.
// Twin of add_process + add_webpage + assign_documentation_to_implementation +
// assign_implementation_to_year_success_indicator + set_evidence_control + set_evidence_rationale.
// Process: hex unique_id (UniqueIdProperty), retired=false. Webpages: raw_text from the live pages fetched
// 2026-10-27 (main content only), raw_text_captured stamped, include_in_report default true.
// No owner: Roger Wen (program lead per the 2024-25 MERLOT report) has no Person node, and add_person needs an
// email and employee_id we do not have. Named in the description instead.
// No satisfies claims: 6.5's requirements are only partly met (see rationale). Idempotent MERGEs.

MERGE (p:Process {title: "Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus)"})
ON CREATE SET p.unique_id = 'cafc6490ea5d490694b56b814da4b06a', p.retired = false
SET p.description = "CSU East Bay's Online Campus runs a course quality assurance program for online and hybrid courses built on Quality Matters, a national peer-review rubric for online course design. The program began in Spring 2015 with Chancellor's Office funding and Provost's Office support. East Bay holds Subscriber-Managed Official Reviews status, so it conducts and certifies its own official course reviews on campus.\n\nFaculty train through Quality Matters or Quality Learning & Teaching workshops (Design Your Online Course, Improve Your Online Course, Applying the QM Rubric). Each faculty member is paired with an eLearning Specialist to prepare the course, and the eLearning Specialists peer review every course before it goes to Quality Matters review. The annual Online and Hybrid Course Quality Transformation Grant pays faculty to redesign a course to the rubric and earn certification.\n\nRoger Wen, Senior Director of the Online Campus, leads the program (2024-25 MERLOT report). Online Campus pays the Quality Matters subscription, review fees and faculty training from its base budget. The campus reports more than 200 certified courses since 2015; in 2024-25, 5 courses were certified and 5 were in progress after Chancellor's Office funding cuts lowered the goal from 10 to 7. The program page names accessibility as one of the program's three standards, and the 2024-25 report lists the accessibility work around it: Verbit captioning, Equidox, UDOIT and TidyUP, and student assistants who remediate course content. Neither source says how accessibility is checked inside a review.";

MERGE (w:Webpage {url: 'https://www.csueastbay.edu/online/faculty-support-services/online-hybrid-course-quality-assurance-program.html'})
ON CREATE SET w.unique_id = replace(randomUUID(), '-', ''), w.include_in_report = true, w.no_longer_exists = false, w.depreciated = false
SET w.name = 'Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (Online Campus)',
    w.description = 'Online Campus program page: what the Quality Matters program is, how faculty train and certify courses, Subscriber-Managed Official Reviews status, and program counts.',
    w.raw_text = "# Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program\n\nCSUEB's Online and Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program is the framework that ensures every online and hybrid course meets a rigorous standard for design, accessibility, and student support. Cal State East Bay is the first - and only - CSU campus with Subscriber-Managed Official Reviews status, which means we conduct and manage our own official course reviews on campus.\n\nQuestions about QA, faculty training, or course certification? Email online@csueastbay.edu or call 510-885-2100 and ask for an eLearning Specialist.\n\n-\n\n-\n\n-\n\n-\n\n-\n\n## Background and what QA means\n\nCSUEB launched the Quality Assurance program at the start of Spring 2015, with funding from the CSU Chancellor's Office and support from the Provost's Office. The work centers around Quality Matters (QM), a nationally recognized organization dedicated to continuous improvement in online course design through peer review.\n\nA QM high-quality course certification has three elements:\n\n- A detailed quality standards rubric for evaluating online and blended course design\n\n- A collegial peer review process conducted by trained reviewers\n\n- Professional development through QM-certified workshops\n\nFaculty are encouraged to take training through Quality Matters (QM) or Quality Learning & Teaching (QLT), work with Online Campus eLearning Specialists, request peer review of their courses, and earn QM or QLT certification as a Quality Online Course.\n\n## Is there a real impact on student learning? Yes.\n\nFaculty who've taken QM workshops or certified their courses have shared how the experience changed their teaching - including how they facilitate face-to-face courses. Reports and studies from CSUEB faculty research associates show evidence of decreased non-passing grades in QA-implemented courses, and faculty perception of the impact is overwhelmingly positive.\n\nWorking with Institutional Effectiveness & Research (IER), we've put historical data (2018-2023) into a public dashboard that tracks the impact of QA on student success.\n\nThe data shows non-passing grades decreased after QA implementation across every demographic group: First-Gen / Non First-Gen, URM / Non-URM, Low Income / Not Low-Income, and more.\n\nView the QA impact dashboard →\n\n## CSUEB's QA program by the numbers\n\n200+\n\ncertified courses since 2015\n\n#1\n\nin California for QM-certified courses\n\n#17\n\nin the nation (Nov 2025)\n\n600+\n\nQM certified workshops completed\n\nCSUEB faculty serve as QM trained personnel across the country:\n\n### 13 Peer Reviewers\n\nCSUEB faculty trained to conduct QM peer reviews of online courses.\n\n### 4 Master Reviewers\n\nSenior reviewers who lead QM official course review teams.\n\n### 6 QM Coordinators\n\nCSUEB-trained coordinators managing the certification process.\n\n### 6 Facilitators\n\nLead the QM-certified workshops we offer to CSUEB faculty.\n\nWhat's special about Subscriber-Managed Official Reviews: CSUEB is the first and only CSU campus with this status. It means we don't have to send our courses to QM HQ for review - we conduct and certify them ourselves on campus, faster and more responsively to faculty needs.\n\n## Workshops and certifications\n\nOnline Campus offers QM-certified workshops on campus. The most-attended sessions are face-to-face workshops designed for faculty new to online teaching as well as those iterating on existing courses.\n\n### Design Your Online Course (DYOC) →\n\nFor faculty building a new online course - covers the QM rubric, alignment, and assessment design.\n\n### Improve Your Online Course (IYOC) →\n\nFor faculty iterating on an existing course toward QM certification.\n\n### Applying the QM Rubric (APPQMR) →\n\nThe foundational QM workshop - introduction to the rubric and review process.\n\nSee full upcoming workshop schedule with Zoom registration →\n\n## Related resources\n\n### Certified Quality Online Courses →\n\nThe full list of CSUEB courses certified through QM (200+ entries).\n\n### Certified QM Reviewers, Coordinators, and Facilitators →\n\nRoster of CSUEB faculty serving in QM-trained roles.\n\n### Quality Matters (national) →\n\nThe national organization, including the public list of QM-certified courses.\n\n### Quality Learning & Teaching (QLT) →\n\nThe CSU systemwide alternative to QM, with similar rigor.\n\nQuestions about QA, training, or course certification? Email online@csueastbay.edu or call 510-885-2100 and ask for an eLearning Specialist. For Canvas help: 1-866-325-0853 (24/7).", w.raw_text_captured = date('2026-10-27');

MERGE (w:Webpage {url: 'https://merlot.org/merlot/viewSite.htm?id=9167737'})
ON CREATE SET w.unique_id = replace(randomUUID(), '-', ''), w.include_in_report = true, w.no_longer_exists = false, w.depreciated = false
SET w.name = '2024-2025 CSU East Bay: Improving a Culture of Quality Assurance (MERLOT QA report)',
    w.description = 'East Bay report to the Chancellor\'s Office QA initiative for 2024-25: goals, lead, funding, peer review before QM submission, certified courses, training completions, and accessibility efforts.',
    w.raw_text = "2024-2025— CSU East Bay— Improving a Culture of Quality Assurance\n\nProposal Summary: Cal State East Bay proposed to have 10 QA certified courses and have 10 faculty members attend a QA training. Due to a reduction of QA funds from the CO, we adjusted our goal of certified courses down to 7. Based on this new number, we were able to exceed both goals.\n\n##\n\nCampus QA Goals\n\nCampus Goals for Quality Assurance\n\nThe main focus of our proposal:\n\n- Certify 10 online and/or hybrid courses through the 2024-2025 CO QA initiative (Later reduced to 7 because of reduced funding from the CO)\n\n- Have 10 faculty members attend some type of QA training.\n\nWe met and exceeded our goals in the following ways:\n\n- 5 courses have received QM certification, with 5 more currently in progress.\n\n- 20 faculty/staff attended some type of QA training with 19 completing the training (see table under Training Completions).\n\nQuality Assurance Lead(s)\n\n- Roger Wen, PhD, Senior Direction of the Online Campus.\n\nSupporting Campus Partners\n\n- Provost and Associate Provost, Academic Affairs\n\n- The Online Campus eLearning Specialists, and LMS Administrators/User Support Consultants\n\n- Office of Faculty Development/Faculty Center for Excellence in Teaching\n\nCampus Commitment Toward Sustainability of QA Efforts\n\nCSUEB’s commitment to complement and sustain QA initiatives is demonstrated by its support for the Online Campus. Since 2015, CSUEB has had a total of 23 cohorts go through various QA initiatives. This has resulted in over 600 faculty receiving QA trainings and over 200 courses becoming QA certified. In the past, the Online Campus worked with Institutional Effectiveness & Research (IER) to create a Preliminary Dashboard to demonstrate the impact of QA on student success.\n\n- The Online Campus is a well-established campus entity with sustained annual funding and robust programming for the development of our faculty. Online Campus also works with Academic Affairs to host and present at Back to the Bay, our annual internal professional development conference, where QA program participants present and disseminate their innovations and best practices.\n\n- CSUEB continues to commit funding to faculty learning communities and the base budget for Online Campus operations. CSUEB’s Online Campus eLearning Specialists also serve as QM managers; we are able to do subscriber-managed QM course reviews, which lowers our course review fee by $200 per course. Online Campus funds the QM annual subscription, course review fees, and the training cost for faculty attend QM certified training every year.\n\nOnline Campus takes every opportunity to showcase our efforts and achievements in regards to QA.\n\nElearning Specialist, Cheryl Saelee\n\npresenting at the 2025 QM Research Conference\n\nElearning Specialist, Shara Cheng presenting a QM Webinar on How CSU East Bay Improved Student Outcomes with CourseArc and QM.\n\nSummary of Previous QA Accomplishments\n\nSince Fall 2015, Cal State East Bay has engaged in a number of efforts focused on QM and Quality Assurance programming, training, and support that contributed to the success of our 2024-2025 efforts. Prior accomplishments include:\n\n-\nTotal of 208 courses (10 certified in 23-24) certified as high quality courses by QM and QLT.\n\n-\n2019: Quality Matters National \"Making a Difference for Students\" Higher Education recognition.\n\n-\nAssessment results: The combined 2016-2017 and 2017-2018 efforts are paid off. When we compared courses taught by QA-participating faculty between Summer 2014 and Summer 2017, the percentage of DWFs in online courses taught by faculty who participated in QA activities [9.60%] is significantly lower than that in courses taught by faculty who have not participated in QA activities [10.47%]. [F(1, 3261)=4.162, p=.041]. We anticipate that the courses taught by participating faculty in the remainder of 2017-2018 as well as 2018-2019 will show similar declines.\n\n-\n11 consecutive years of the annual Online and Hybrid Course Quality Transformation Grant Program (QA) since 2014 - 2015.\n\n-\n2014-2015 Online and Hybrid Course Quality Transformation Grant Program (QA).\n\n- 2014-2015 Teaching with Technology Faculty Learning Community.\n\n2014: Summer Institute: Journey to Excellence in Online Education (QOLT introduced).\n\n-\n2015-2016 QOLT Campus Nominee, Kevin Kaatz, Department of History.\n\n-\n2015 College Coordinators for Quality Assurance Faculty Learning Community (QM-based).\n\n-\n2014-2015 QOLT Awardee, Kevin Kaatz, Department of History.\n\n##\n\nQuality Assurance Results\n\nTraining Completions\n\nThe following table summarizes all Cal State East Bay faculty and staff that have taken QA training during the 2024-2025 academic year.\n\nTraining # of Completions\n\nImproving Your Online Course (IYOC)6\n\nAI Tools for Teaching & Learning13\n\nCourse Peer Review and Course Certifications\n\nEach faculty member is paired with an eLearning Specialist to collaborate to get the course ready for review. The eLearning Specialists conducts peer reviews of all courses before they are submitted to QM.\n\nAt Cal State East Bay, there are currently 15 eligible QM Peer and Master Reviewers:\n\n- Amy June Rowley\n\n- Andrew Wong\n\n- Beverly Bondad-Brown\n\n- Cheryl Saelee\n\n- Chinglih Jan\n\n- Erick Kong\n\n- Kimberly Kim\n\n- Michelle Rippy\n\n- Roger Wen\n\n- Serena Kohgadai\n\n- Shara Cheng\n\n- Ashley Skylar (Master Reviewer)\n\n- James Thomas (Master Reviewer)\n\n- Keelin Jardin (Master Reviewer)\n\n- Kevin Kaatz (Master Reviewer)\n\nThe table below shows the 10 Quality Matters Certified courses or in process for 2024-2025 Academic Year.\n\nCourse ID\n\nCourse Name\n\nInstructor\n\nCRJ 375\n\nFamily Violence and the Criminal Justice System\n\nDawna Komorosky\n\nHIST 301\n\nHistory Writing Workshop\n\nJessica Weiss\n\nACCT 311\n\nIntermediate Financial Accounting I\n\nTina Lang\n\nSPED 602A\n\nAutism Spectrum Disorders: Characteristics and Etiology\n\nMeaghan McCollow\n\nINFO 210\n\nIntroduction to Searching for and Evaluating Information\n\nBrittany Butler\n\nMGMT 360\n\nOperations Management\n\nInkyu Kim\n\nSOC 301W\n\nBuilding the Online Environment\n\nAaron Miller\n\nSW 601\n\nRace, Gender, & Inequality\n\nJill Yang\n\nMLL 334\n\nDe'VIA and Deaf Artists\n\nAmy June Rowley\n\nHDEV 381\n\nInterdisciplinary Perspectives Lifespan Human Development\n\nRoxanne Rashedi\n\nDevelopment of Campus QA Resources\n\nList of Resources Developed\n\n- Continue to develop faculty and students help guides with Screensteps.\nSyllabus information sheet that contains information about what to include according to the CSUEB Policy on Course Syllabus Information and to meet Quality Matter Standards.\n\n-\nDesigned syllabus templates available for faculty\n\n-\nMaintaining a master university template that meets the QA standards\n\n-\nVideo tutorials for Canvas\n\n-\nRegular faculty workshops on course design related topics.\n\n-\nContinue to update Online Student Support and Services\n\n-\nContinue to update Learning Technologies and Services Guide\n\nA video on how to create and use rubrics in Canvas, which assures course quality and addresses QM standards.\n\nAccessibility/UDL Efforts\n\nDue to the U.S. Department of Justice requiring all campuses to ensure that all digital course materials are accessible to individuals with disabilities (Title II of the ADA), CSUEB has taken significant steps to increase accessibility in courses, campus training, and campus tools.\n\n- Online Campus participated in the Echo Labs pilot program organized by the Chancellor's Office for captioning videos in order to meet the standards for Title II.\n\n- CSUEB purchased a subscription with Verbit Unlimited to continue captioning videos to meet the accessibility standards for Title II.\n\n- With the help of the CO, we’ve acquired Equidox to remediate PDFs to meet the accessibility standards for Title II.\n\n- Via CO MEA, we’ve acquired tools such as UDOIT and TidyUp to meet the accessibility standards for Title II.\n\n- Online Campus created our own Accessibility Webpage to share Title II updates and resources with faculty.\n\n- Online Campus is hiring more student assistants to help remediate course content.\n\n- We’ve increased our focus on accessibility resources in our weekly newsletter to faculty and staff.\n\n- Offered accessibility training workshops to faculty in an effort to establish an inclusive campus community.\n\n- Continue to train student assistants on using UDOIT, TidyUP, ABBY, and Equidox.\n\nAn Online Campus video on how to add alt-text to images in Canvas.\n\nDissemination of QA Efforts— Conferences\n\n- Drivers of Educational Technology California Higher Ed (DET/CHE) Conference 2024 - Roger Wen, Presenter, Link to Presentation\n\n- Power Your Purpose, CSUEB’s Women in Leadership Forum 2024 - Leading with AI, Cheryl Saelee, Presenter, Link to Presentation\n\n- QM Research Conference 2025 - Cheryl Saelee, Presenter, Link to Presentation.\n\n- 2025 QM Webinar - How California State University East Bay Improved Student Outcomes with CourseArc and QM. Shara Cheng, Presenter. Link to Webinar\n\nNext Steps for QA Efforts\n\n- Continue to encourage and support more faculty members to attend QA training.\n\n- Identify and certify more online or hybrid courses in 2025 - 2026 through supporting Cohort 24 (OHQT Grant).\n\n- Seek additional funding to elevate research and progress.", w.raw_text_captured = date('2026-10-27');

MATCH (p:Process {title: "Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus)"}), (w:Webpage {url: 'https://www.csueastbay.edu/online/faculty-support-services/online-hybrid-course-quality-assurance-program.html'})
MERGE (p)-[d:is_documented_by]->(w)
ON CREATE SET d.added_date = date('2026-10-27'), d.added_by = 'a1d223af-c7aa-466b-bf54-47f0a199696d', d.included_in_years = [], d.excluded_from_years = [];

MATCH (p:Process {title: "Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus)"}), (w:Webpage {url: 'https://merlot.org/merlot/viewSite.htm?id=9167737'})
MERGE (p)-[d:is_documented_by]->(w)
ON CREATE SET d.added_date = date('2026-10-27'), d.added_by = 'a1d223af-c7aa-466b-bf54-47f0a199696d', d.included_in_years = [], d.excluded_from_years = [];

MATCH (p:Process {title: "Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus)"}), (y:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.5-ins-csueb'})
MERGE (p)-[r:is_evidence_for]->(y)
ON CREATE SET r.strength = 2, r.control = 'internal', r.satisfies = []
SET r.rationale = coalesce(r.rationale, "This is East Bay's course redesign process: faculty redesign an online or hybrid course to the Quality Matters rubric with an eLearning Specialist, who peer reviews the course before official review, and the course is then certified. Online Campus funds it from its base budget and a named director leads it. It answers this indicator partly. It covers opt-in online and hybrid courses, about 10 a year, not every redesign, and neither source shows where accessibility is checked against WCAG 2.1 AA during the review or reports accessibility measures before and after redesign.");

MATCH (p:Process {title: "Online & Hybrid Course Quality Assurance Program (CSUEB Online Campus)"}), (y:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.12-ins-csueb'})
MERGE (p)-[r:is_evidence_for]->(y)
ON CREATE SET r.strength = 1, r.control = 'internal', r.satisfies = []
SET r.rationale = coalesce(r.rationale, "The program's Quality Matters workshops are a standing part of faculty development at East Bay, and the 2024-25 report lists accessibility training workshops and accessibility items in the weekly faculty newsletter alongside them. Accessibility is one of the program's standards rather than its subject, so the link is indirect.");
